1
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:26,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in regards to rituals, would you say that they are useless in one's life?

2
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:26,591
[SPEAKER_01]: A ritual?

3
00:00:27,453 --> 00:00:31,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it depends on the purpose.

4
00:00:33,025 --> 00:00:39,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps you'd like to highlight your intentions, say, for, towards enlightenment, intentions towards renunciation.

5
00:00:39,791 --> 00:00:43,714
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, of course, I mean, towards enlightenment, they're completely useless.

6
00:00:44,535 --> 00:00:48,819
[SPEAKER_02]: But to say they're completely useless for an average person's life, that's not true either.

7
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:57,987
[SPEAKER_02]: In the sense that, if there were no rituals, many people would have no, would have had no sense of routine.

8
00:00:59,002 --> 00:01:03,263
[SPEAKER_02]: No sense of duty or a certain, like, repetition.

9
00:01:03,863 --> 00:01:11,524
[SPEAKER_02]: And then rituals make them stick to it out of that, eh... kind of primordial anxiety and fear towards your existence.

10
00:01:11,564 --> 00:01:13,545
[SPEAKER_02]: You turn towards rituals and at least you do them.

11
00:01:13,945 --> 00:01:15,605
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, it's fundamentally the wrong view.

12
00:01:15,665 --> 00:01:21,166
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why if you were to use that as a vehicle to enlightenment, it's just impossible.

13
00:01:21,226 --> 00:01:22,426
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually an impediment.

14
00:01:24,247 --> 00:01:24,907
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a fetter.

15
00:01:28,107 --> 00:01:31,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Like coming of age rituals that different societies have.

16
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:36,073
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it's just symbolism and whatnot.

17
00:01:36,113 --> 00:01:37,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Rituals towards like having a shrine.

18
00:01:39,095 --> 00:01:45,060
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, see, now you're coming into more specific, more specific type, and that's where you have to ask yourself why.

19
00:01:46,461 --> 00:01:50,504
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, cultural sort of rituals in society are part of the culture.

20
00:01:50,524 --> 00:01:51,605
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing to do with anything.

21
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:52,845
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just how it evolved.

22
00:01:53,927 --> 00:02:00,009
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, if you're having rituals in regard to your own shrine, you ask yourself why.

23
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:05,210
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it because you're not practicing towards the enlightenment?

24
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:08,971
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, what difference does it make if you're bowing to the shrine?

25
00:02:09,131 --> 00:02:10,031
[SPEAKER_02]: What are you doing with it?

26
00:02:10,931 --> 00:02:12,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you expecting something to happen to you?

27
00:02:12,892 --> 00:02:14,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Something to be given to you?

28
00:02:15,253 --> 00:02:20,354
[SPEAKER_02]: Blessings from something or someone through your idol worship?

29
00:02:22,869 --> 00:02:28,155
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... so, for somebody who's practicing the dhamma, there really is no room for any rituals.

30
00:02:28,175 --> 00:02:38,887
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you... if you keep maintaining the need of rituals in your life, to that extent, you're not practicing towards the right view, because you're depending on this external magical belief.

31
00:02:42,422 --> 00:02:51,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's basically placing responsibility on the performance of ritual, as opposed to taking up responsibility yourself, in which case you feel it very differently.

32
00:02:52,209 --> 00:03:02,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not about what you're meant to do, what the signs have shown you to do, it's what you chose to do, which means any second you can choose otherwise.

33
00:03:02,737 --> 00:03:03,718
[SPEAKER_02]: And you feel that edge.

34
00:03:04,539 --> 00:03:05,519
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's just necessary.

35
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:15,068
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're avoiding taking on that kind of weight of responsibility, that's when you sort of give in and put responsibility onto rituals.

36
00:03:15,349 --> 00:03:17,030
[SPEAKER_02]: And to that extent, you won't be practicing.

37
00:03:17,391 --> 00:03:22,055
[SPEAKER_01]: What about protocols in society?

38
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:24,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Oh, they're just protocols.

39
00:03:24,558 --> 00:03:26,800
[SPEAKER_02]: They're just empty motions you go through.

40
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:27,961
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't need to mean anything.

41
00:03:28,608 --> 00:03:30,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you disregard all protocols and manners?

42
00:03:30,709 --> 00:03:34,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you have to be careful, because protocols and manners mean a lot to other people.

43
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:39,392
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you disregard it, you're going to be harassed, on that account.

44
00:03:40,472 --> 00:03:54,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is exactly why there's so many of those, how do I say, small, smaller rules, that are completely irrelevant to the practice of dhamma, but we still have them as monks, because they kind of originated organically, like with interactions with the society.

45
00:03:55,359 --> 00:03:56,720
[SPEAKER_02]: But there's nothing to do with anything.

46
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,361
[SPEAKER_02]: It was just like the certain norms in the current society.

47
00:04:00,401 --> 00:04:02,322
[SPEAKER_02]: They complain about certain things.

48
00:04:02,342 --> 00:04:08,845
[SPEAKER_02]: And yeah, the Buddha could have sort of explained to them that it was completely wrong and it's a wrong view, but it wouldn't have changed much.

49
00:04:10,466 --> 00:04:13,207
[SPEAKER_02]: Because people wouldn't have abandoned those views.

50
00:04:13,487 --> 00:04:19,550
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, when you look at it practically, it only resulted in monks being harassed and hassled on account of upsetting people.

51
00:04:20,786 --> 00:04:27,509
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't mean you don't draw a line when it comes to dhamma, and you just go with whatever people deem... whatever people want to hear.

52
00:04:27,929 --> 00:04:32,291
[SPEAKER_02]: But certain things that are not impediment to the dhamma, either way...

53
00:04:32,391 --> 00:04:36,873
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether you do it or not do it, makes no difference if you practice the dhamma, but makes a difference to the society you live in.

54
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:41,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you do what the society expects you, so you'll be left alone, so you can practice in peace.

55
00:04:42,775 --> 00:04:48,858
[SPEAKER_02]: But, of course, if the society demands you to get married or something, well, that's directly against the practice of dhamma, so you just will never

56
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:53,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if the society is very upset.

57
00:04:55,223 --> 00:05:02,006
[SPEAKER_02]: But if society demands you to wear shoes in town because they can't stand the sight of a barefoot person, that's fine.

58
00:05:02,066 --> 00:05:02,807
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes no difference.

59
00:05:03,787 --> 00:05:06,148
[SPEAKER_02]: And the only difference gonna be is how much harassment you're gonna get.

60
00:05:06,929 --> 00:05:14,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Because respect... respecting your elders,

61
00:05:15,741 --> 00:05:17,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, there's nothing wrong with that.

62
00:05:18,903 --> 00:05:22,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's a bit more, you know, there is some meaning behind that.

63
00:05:23,807 --> 00:05:29,131
[SPEAKER_02]: You should respect them, because without them you wouldn't have... you wouldn't be where you are.

64
00:05:31,032 --> 00:05:38,438
[SPEAKER_02]: And respecting elders in general, again, it maintains a certain standard of relationship with others, that then others can learn from.

65
00:05:40,389 --> 00:05:47,055
[SPEAKER_02]: If you start disregarding elders and teaching others to disregard the elders, then, yeah, the values of respecting elders will be eroded.

66
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:51,620
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't mean you agree that elders always know the best for you.

67
00:05:52,501 --> 00:05:53,562
[SPEAKER_02]: You even know the best for them.

68
00:05:54,282 --> 00:05:59,047
[SPEAKER_02]: But in the terms of the way you would relate to another person, yeah, you respect their older.

69
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:02,263
[SPEAKER_02]: And same with monk seniority.

70
00:06:02,343 --> 00:06:14,192
[SPEAKER_02]: According to the original, vinaya seniority is just so the people will dwell in concord, so we don't all go at the same time and grab the pot with food and fight over who's gonna serve first.

71
00:06:14,851 --> 00:06:14,991
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

72
00:06:15,171 --> 00:06:17,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Certain order means certain harmony.

73
00:06:18,193 --> 00:06:19,854
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's as far as it goes.

74
00:06:20,074 --> 00:06:29,900
[SPEAKER_02]: It does not mean that somebody who's more senior has a say over you, or has a say to tell you what to do and knows better, unless you took them as a teacher, because you do see that they know more than you do.

75
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:35,503
[SPEAKER_02]: But in itself, as a kind of institutional order, that's it.

76
00:06:35,583 --> 00:06:36,883
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on a very worldly level.

77
00:06:37,484 --> 00:06:39,105
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you go first because you've been here longer.

78
00:06:39,125 --> 00:06:39,525
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

79
00:06:40,731 --> 00:06:48,362
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean that your vote or your say weighs more than mine, because you've been here longer, when it comes to dhamma or to the saṅgha methods.

80
00:06:50,198 --> 00:06:55,899
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's often... well, often... yeah, I guess, often gets sort of blurred in between.

81
00:06:55,919 --> 00:07:03,141
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by default, by being more senior means you know more, or you certainly have more say over those who are not as senior.

82
00:07:03,661 --> 00:07:05,101
[SPEAKER_02]: They must do what you tell them to do.

83
00:07:05,441 --> 00:07:06,582
[SPEAKER_02]: That's simply not the case.

84
00:07:07,062 --> 00:07:15,263
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless they took on that relationship of a teacher, in which case, a junior person can be a teacher, if he's taken as a teacher by somebody else.

85
00:07:15,863 --> 00:07:17,804
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a completely different type of relationship.

86
00:07:20,455 --> 00:07:23,617
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when people rebel against seniority, that's usually what they rebel against.

87
00:07:25,778 --> 00:07:28,280
[SPEAKER_02]: But they don't necessarily see that, because the lines were blurred.

88
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:30,161
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't rebel against...

89
00:07:30,321 --> 00:07:38,906
[SPEAKER_02]: They shouldn't be rebelling against just a norm of respecting elders, and elders walking first and so on, or the ones who are more senior.

90
00:07:39,306 --> 00:07:45,890
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, they should rebel elders sort of assuming wisdom, just because they've been here longer, and they assume that they know more than you do.

91
00:07:47,893 --> 00:07:49,274
[SPEAKER_02]: But even against that, you don't have to rebel.

92
00:07:49,814 --> 00:07:52,735
[SPEAKER_02]: You just pay no attention to it.

93
00:07:54,116 --> 00:07:56,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your parents aren't exactly the nicest of people...

94
00:07:57,217 --> 00:07:57,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

95
00:07:57,558 --> 00:08:07,983
[SPEAKER_02]: You respect them as your parents, but that doesn't necessarily mean you... you... you... you listen to them in a... in a... when they tell you what to do, because they generally don't have a clue.

96
00:08:09,643 --> 00:08:10,644
[SPEAKER_02]: Or they might not have a clue.

97
00:08:11,454 --> 00:08:18,456
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't dismiss it, but once you fully understood what you want to do, what you're doing, why it is good, and the parents are saying, telling you the opposite.

98
00:08:18,476 --> 00:08:20,236
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you should get married and have children and stuff.

99
00:08:20,256 --> 00:08:21,537
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you realize that that's ignorant.

100
00:08:21,617 --> 00:08:23,457
[SPEAKER_02]: That's just their craving and desire talking.

101
00:08:24,398 --> 00:08:26,278
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't disrespect them on account of it.

102
00:08:27,419 --> 00:08:28,639
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no need for disrespect.

103
00:08:31,943 --> 00:08:35,824
[SPEAKER_01]: What type of work should a person do, in your opinion?

104
00:08:36,004 --> 00:08:38,405
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the best kind of work?

105
00:08:39,485 --> 00:08:41,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, who are we talking about?

106
00:08:41,886 --> 00:08:42,946
[SPEAKER_01]: For a layperson, sir.

107
00:08:43,666 --> 00:08:44,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a master.

108
00:08:44,366 --> 00:08:44,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, right.

109
00:08:44,707 --> 00:08:46,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, any work that doesn't go against the five precepts.

110
00:08:54,269 --> 00:08:57,530
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's... what would you say is the purpose of your work?

111
00:08:58,831 --> 00:09:00,311
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, just sustaining your life.

112
00:09:04,607 --> 00:09:07,549
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's about it.

113
00:09:07,689 --> 00:09:14,052
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you find a certain balance between how much you want and how much you actually need.

114
00:09:15,533 --> 00:09:18,635
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you work to meet your needs and maybe get extra just in case.

115
00:09:19,455 --> 00:09:25,079
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't work for the sake of gain that you can't even possibly practically benefit from.

116
00:09:25,899 --> 00:09:27,180
[SPEAKER_02]: Nor can you benefit others.

117
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:32,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is when people just keep acting out of ideals of wealth and

118
00:09:33,523 --> 00:09:38,804
[SPEAKER_02]: In acquiring things, well, like in reality, you can't even... you can't even use it all up.

119
00:09:40,645 --> 00:09:45,406
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you have family and children, yeah, that's, of course, something you have to work for, to support them.

120
00:09:45,546 --> 00:09:51,627
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't, means you... you don't really have to work much, if you lower your needs and live simply.

121
00:09:51,667 --> 00:09:56,948
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to keep your ambition in check and your cravings towards gain.

122
00:09:58,469 --> 00:10:00,530
[SPEAKER_02]: There was access, there was luxury, there was comfort.

123
00:10:01,190 --> 00:10:05,192
[SPEAKER_02]: All those things need to be paid for, which means you will need to work for all those things.

124
00:10:05,292 --> 00:10:11,135
[SPEAKER_02]: But most people end up working for a lot, lot more than they actually even want.

125
00:10:12,396 --> 00:10:22,821
[SPEAKER_02]: But because they never stop and think about what they actually want, let alone what they really need, they just end up going with a craving towards more and being very unhappy on account of it.

126
00:10:23,662 --> 00:10:26,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they didn't clarify where the line is for them.

127
00:10:27,003 --> 00:10:28,164
[SPEAKER_02]: They didn't draw the line, really.

128
00:10:29,433 --> 00:10:30,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Wisdom is the key.

129
00:10:30,634 --> 00:10:35,996
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, any form of self-reflection is a prerequisite for wisdom, for understanding.

130
00:10:36,536 --> 00:10:42,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Paired with wholesome, of course.

131
00:10:42,939 --> 00:10:46,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... because a lot of people want to say that work is suffering, you know.

132
00:10:47,021 --> 00:10:47,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is.

133
00:10:47,521 --> 00:10:47,981
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, sure.

134
00:10:48,001 --> 00:10:51,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Working every day, eight hours a day, whatever.

135
00:10:52,603 --> 00:10:56,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the most obvious example is when people start having families.

136
00:10:59,185 --> 00:11:03,449
[SPEAKER_02]: You pair up, you have children and so on, and they don't really think it through.

137
00:11:04,850 --> 00:11:07,972
[SPEAKER_02]: They just act out of desire to have children.

138
00:11:08,013 --> 00:11:10,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Desire of creating a family.

139
00:11:10,254 --> 00:11:17,200
[SPEAKER_02]: But they ignore the weight of responsibility and the burden of work that's implied in it.

140
00:11:17,981 --> 00:11:19,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you have one child, you have to work for it.

141
00:11:19,622 --> 00:11:21,424
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have two, you have to work even more.

142
00:11:21,444 --> 00:11:25,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Three, four children means you have to work a lot.

143
00:11:27,370 --> 00:11:38,137
[SPEAKER_02]: And that also means that if you don't have work, if you keep finding different work, it means you have to work for very little, because you have no... you don't have much leverage.

144
00:11:38,917 --> 00:11:39,578
[SPEAKER_02]: You need work.

145
00:11:39,678 --> 00:11:43,200
[SPEAKER_02]: You need any form of earning, because you have three children to support.

146
00:11:45,261 --> 00:11:50,765
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, you can't blame anybody else for that, because you created as children.

147
00:11:54,938 --> 00:11:55,839
[SPEAKER_01]: One shouldn't avoid...

148
00:11:56,299 --> 00:11:56,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

149
00:11:56,959 --> 00:11:58,420
[SPEAKER_01]: One shouldn't avoid work.

150
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:00,461
[SPEAKER_02]: No, of course not.

151
00:12:01,982 --> 00:12:03,662
[SPEAKER_02]: But one shouldn't be... Life of leisure.

152
00:12:03,762 --> 00:12:03,943
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

153
00:12:04,323 --> 00:12:06,104
[SPEAKER_02]: One shouldn't be ignorant in regard to it either.

154
00:12:06,964 --> 00:12:07,644
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

155
00:12:07,684 --> 00:12:08,285
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a...

156
00:12:09,091 --> 00:12:10,692
[SPEAKER_02]: You must work for something.

157
00:12:11,313 --> 00:12:14,295
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, even as monks, that's how often it was in the suttas.

158
00:12:14,876 --> 00:12:17,558
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the carpenters, they work with wood.

159
00:12:18,158 --> 00:12:20,060
[SPEAKER_02]: The arrowsmiths, they make arrows.

160
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:22,422
[SPEAKER_02]: And the monks, they tame themselves.

161
00:12:22,502 --> 00:12:23,463
[SPEAKER_02]: They practice restraint.

162
00:12:23,703 --> 00:12:24,584
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's their work.

163
00:12:24,984 --> 00:12:27,466
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where they get the food and people give them offerings.

164
00:12:28,287 --> 00:12:34,812
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in a way, it's like the most wholesome way of transaction, so to speak.

165
00:12:35,798 --> 00:12:41,119
[SPEAKER_02]: People, by making, by giving, they make merit and also they can learn dhamma.

166
00:12:41,339 --> 00:12:42,860
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is no fixed price.

167
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:45,400
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, you have no wage.

168
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:48,281
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sometimes you might not get anything, sometimes you might get too much.

169
00:12:49,901 --> 00:12:50,801
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you just sort of...

170
00:12:50,821 --> 00:12:52,142
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not your concern.

171
00:12:52,642 --> 00:12:56,623
[SPEAKER_02]: Your concern is to do that work for yourself, not for the gain.

172
00:12:57,783 --> 00:13:02,944
[SPEAKER_02]: If the gain comes to the extent of having your basic requisites covered,

173
00:13:03,563 --> 00:13:04,123
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's fine.

174
00:13:04,344 --> 00:13:06,085
[SPEAKER_02]: If it comes more, you might share with other monks.

175
00:13:06,365 --> 00:13:06,725
[SPEAKER_02]: That's fine.

176
00:13:06,745 --> 00:13:08,447
[SPEAKER_02]: If none of that, that's also fine.

177
00:13:09,608 --> 00:13:11,949
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that different when somebody works for a wage.

178
00:13:12,430 --> 00:13:14,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I'll do this, you pay me this much.

179
00:13:14,972 --> 00:13:19,295
[SPEAKER_02]: Just, it's more wholesome in a way, because people will just donate, so to speak.

180
00:13:19,315 --> 00:13:20,256
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't put a price on it.

181
00:13:20,976 --> 00:13:21,977
[SPEAKER_02]: But the principle is the same.

182
00:13:23,338 --> 00:13:26,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you do, you have to work to a degree or something.

183
00:13:26,901 --> 00:13:31,805
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the point is that most people in the world, not the monks, they don't

184
00:13:32,903 --> 00:13:37,949
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't think through what they want in their life, what they need in their life.

185
00:13:38,890 --> 00:13:43,535
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, they just end up, like everybody else, just going with the sensuality, with the sensual desires.

186
