230
00:16:44,819 --> 00:16:46,920
[SPEAKER_02]: With the presence of a nutriment, this is present.

231
00:16:47,781 --> 00:16:49,803
[SPEAKER_02]: When nutriment is not there, this cannot be either.

232
00:16:50,323 --> 00:16:51,524
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that dependence of it.

233
00:16:51,864 --> 00:16:54,767
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't necessarily go and investigate the content of the nutrient.

234
00:16:56,716 --> 00:17:00,799
[SPEAKER_02]: You look at the principle of dependence upon the nutrient.

235
00:17:01,939 --> 00:17:05,241
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is pretty much the most fundamental thing.

236
00:17:07,002 --> 00:17:12,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why the Buddha said, the one who sees patitsaṃbhāra sees the dhamma, the one who sees the dhamma sees patitsaṃbhāra.

237
00:17:12,966 --> 00:17:13,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Or maybe Sariputra said that.

238
00:17:14,006 --> 00:17:16,168
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

239
00:17:16,368 --> 00:17:23,932
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if you don't see it on that fundamental level, well, you can see it on another, almost as equally fundamental, which is the name and form.

240
00:17:24,472 --> 00:17:25,473
[SPEAKER_02]: The diet, the two.

241
00:17:26,536 --> 00:17:29,258
[SPEAKER_02]: And it... that's everything that you can conceive of.

242
00:17:29,438 --> 00:17:30,799
[SPEAKER_02]: It's within name and form.

243
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:32,781
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no outside of it.

244
00:17:33,001 --> 00:17:37,745
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no... there's no behind it, that you can also be aware of, that does not partake in name and form.

245
00:17:37,765 --> 00:17:45,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you can be aware... whatever you can be conscious of in the most remote, peripheral, ambiguous sense, it's within name and form.

246
00:17:47,112 --> 00:17:48,013
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the name and form.

247
00:17:49,374 --> 00:17:54,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever has a nature to appear, no matter how subtly, it's the name and form.

248
00:17:55,683 --> 00:18:02,866
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you understand the principle of appearance, as in the principle of the name and form of that diet, you've understood everything.

249
00:18:02,906 --> 00:18:07,307
[SPEAKER_02]: If you experience dispassion towards it, disenchantment, as the Buddha describes it.

250
00:18:08,408 --> 00:18:14,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, if you fail to see it on that level, well, you have another chance of... in regard to the three types of feelings.

251
00:18:15,770 --> 00:18:19,392
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's another point of convergence, so to speak.

252
00:18:22,213 --> 00:18:22,713
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on...

253
00:18:24,418 --> 00:18:25,699
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of how you feel.

254
00:18:27,239 --> 00:18:29,520
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of how you feel, that's where you crave.

255
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:30,541
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you crave.

256
00:18:30,961 --> 00:18:31,721
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you crave.

257
00:18:32,241 --> 00:18:33,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Through not understanding the feeling.

258
00:18:34,622 --> 00:18:44,546
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by understanding the principle of craving in regard to the three types of feeling, by experiencing dispassion in regard to the three types of feeling, disenchantment, you're completely free.

259
00:18:46,387 --> 00:18:48,068
[SPEAKER_02]: All three things result in the same, obviously.

260
00:18:49,288 --> 00:18:50,609
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just, eh... how...

261
00:18:53,325 --> 00:18:57,470
[SPEAKER_02]: The difference is how deeply understanding sort of contemplation pertains to.

262
00:18:58,090 --> 00:19:02,815
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't matter even if it's not to the most fundamental level.

263
00:19:03,877 --> 00:19:09,343
[SPEAKER_02]: As long as the same principle of dispassion is developed in regard to the relevant things, that's all that matters.

264
00:19:29,139 --> 00:19:31,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you can you can say that.

265
00:19:32,381 --> 00:19:40,204
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, you want to... you don't want, as I said, you don't want to be just looking at the particular content of that nutriment.

266
00:19:40,304 --> 00:19:42,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be looking at the principle of dependence.

267
00:19:42,805 --> 00:19:44,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say

268
00:19:59,926 --> 00:20:01,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Your life depends on eating food.

269
00:20:03,448 --> 00:20:26,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, there are many different things within that, that can be standing for the same principle, such as having the healthy body, that can still process and digest food, having your intestines working properly, having the right hormonal balance and everything else, because if any of those things get messed up, you won't be able to be... well, the food won't be making much difference to you.

270
00:20:29,087 --> 00:20:33,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, you can be very healthy, but if there isn't a suitable food, same.

271
00:20:34,332 --> 00:20:45,082
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the nutrient... the principle nutrient means, depending upon something, you, in your core, depending upon something, that is not yours, that cannot be yours.

272
00:20:46,083 --> 00:20:50,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can be accruing all the food you want, but you have no say.

273
00:20:50,895 --> 00:21:09,583
[SPEAKER_02]: over the the molecular operations of your organs, whether they're still healthy, whether they're still absorbing things correctly, or you're having some some disease growing in you, or cancer, that again, it's going to render the body useless, so no amount of good and healthy food will make you alive anymore.

274
00:21:11,116 --> 00:21:11,756
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

275
00:21:11,776 --> 00:21:15,297
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to discern the dependence in your core.

276
00:21:15,337 --> 00:21:21,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like explain it in a medical or scientific or psychological manner, saying, yes, food results in this.

277
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:22,020
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

278
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:26,401
[SPEAKER_02]: See that this, you start with this, which is you are, the present experience as a whole.

279
00:21:26,961 --> 00:21:32,463
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that, in its heart, there is a dependence upon things that are not in your control.

280
00:21:33,343 --> 00:21:35,044
[SPEAKER_02]: That's in the heart of your very being.

281
00:21:36,338 --> 00:21:42,100
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't start by... like... you don't start from the... you don't start from the start.

282
00:21:43,361 --> 00:21:49,964
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to reverse back into the... into the... the correct understanding of what food is.

283
00:21:49,984 --> 00:21:52,465
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't start like food makes me this.

284
00:21:53,185 --> 00:21:53,345
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

285
00:21:53,365 --> 00:21:56,106
[SPEAKER_02]: You start with this depends on food.

286
00:21:57,033 --> 00:21:59,594
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is the opposite direction.

287
00:21:59,674 --> 00:22:02,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's true, yeah, the food makes you alive, makes you healthy, makes everything.

288
00:22:02,876 --> 00:22:06,738
[SPEAKER_02]: But that is not seen in that personal manner.

289
00:22:06,758 --> 00:22:09,600
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not seeing the principle of that personal dependence.

290
00:22:10,040 --> 00:22:16,644
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just explaining to yourself how, sort of, your body works when it comes to food and nutrients.

291
00:22:17,564 --> 00:22:19,145
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not the insight.

292
00:22:19,265 --> 00:22:20,246
[SPEAKER_02]: That's an explanation.

293
00:22:21,106 --> 00:22:25,509
[SPEAKER_02]: In order to see their dependence, you start with what you have, which is this body here.

294
00:22:27,096 --> 00:22:30,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever it is, healthy or partially healthy or sick, doesn't matter.

295
00:22:30,660 --> 00:22:31,861
[SPEAKER_01]: The center of the universe.

296
00:22:32,001 --> 00:22:33,322
[SPEAKER_02]: That is your center of the universe.

297
00:22:33,742 --> 00:22:34,783
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your point of view.

298
00:22:35,024 --> 00:22:35,804
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your situation.

299
00:22:35,824 --> 00:22:37,246
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your world, as the Buddha said.

300
00:22:37,306 --> 00:22:42,290
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no world outside of this body less than two meters tall.

301
00:22:42,751 --> 00:22:43,071
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

302
00:22:43,091 --> 00:22:44,792
[SPEAKER_02]: The whole universe is contained within it.

303
00:22:45,433 --> 00:22:46,894
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not in some metaphysical sense.

304
00:22:46,934 --> 00:22:51,639
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just exactly... No matter how far you travel, it's through your body that you get to experience the world.

305
00:22:51,659 --> 00:22:52,980
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the body is your world.

306
00:22:54,523 --> 00:23:18,985
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to start with that and then, without abandoning that, without jumping back on to psychologizing or explaining it, within your situation of your body, of your present experience as such, you try to discern within that the dependence on the phenomenon of nutrient, of support, different than itself.

307
00:23:20,423 --> 00:23:21,783
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the definition of a nutriment.

308
00:23:21,923 --> 00:23:26,184
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't actually contemplate the food, cornflakes, bread.

309
00:23:26,285 --> 00:23:26,465
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

310
00:23:26,985 --> 00:23:28,885
[SPEAKER_02]: You just look at the principle of dependence.

311
00:23:29,485 --> 00:23:30,486
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the definition.

312
00:23:30,606 --> 00:23:34,747
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the nutriment, understanding the nutriment, means understanding the principle of dependence.

313
00:23:36,127 --> 00:23:37,367
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is your entire world.

314
00:23:37,587 --> 00:23:38,788
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all you can ever have.

315
00:23:39,088 --> 00:23:40,248
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't step outside of it.

316
00:23:41,488 --> 00:23:47,130
[SPEAKER_02]: And you see that in its heart, depend upon the whole domain of things,

317
00:23:48,418 --> 00:23:53,421
[SPEAKER_02]: That cannot be controlled by this domain of my body.

318
00:23:54,761 --> 00:24:00,604
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that sense, you see that your whole life depends upon something else, that it's not in your control.

319
00:24:02,385 --> 00:24:04,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is not yours, not you.

320
00:24:05,227 --> 00:24:05,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot be you.

321
00:24:07,234 --> 00:24:20,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can be, you can be, as I said, you can be collecting and hoarding up all the food so you never go hungry, but that's aiming at a different level, at a wrong level.

322
00:24:21,237 --> 00:24:25,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it doesn't matter how much you have or how much you don't have.

323
00:24:25,118 --> 00:24:28,339
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is that you cannot change the fact that you depend on it.

324
00:24:29,159 --> 00:24:29,999
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the main point.

325
00:24:31,079 --> 00:24:34,200
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can have all the food for the rest of your life, never to go hungry.

326
00:24:35,030 --> 00:24:42,516
[SPEAKER_02]: That, again, that doesn't bring you any closer towards understanding the nature of depending upon food in the first place.

327
00:24:42,676 --> 00:24:45,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you have the food or not, has nothing to do with it.

328
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:52,304
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to do with depending upon that which is not you, which cannot be you, which cannot be assimilated into you.

329
00:24:55,186 --> 00:25:01,972
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not incidental that eating sort of mirrors that principle of assimilation, of possession.

330
00:25:03,239 --> 00:25:07,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're trying to regain the control of your very being through eating.

331
00:25:07,641 --> 00:25:11,123
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why any form of sensuality ends up being manifested as consuming.

332
00:25:12,143 --> 00:25:15,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Visually, audibly, tactile and so on.

333
00:25:15,225 --> 00:25:17,326
[SPEAKER_02]: Trying to take in the world.

334
00:25:17,346 --> 00:25:21,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Try to take... try to repossess it, that upon which you actually depend.

335
00:25:22,848 --> 00:25:28,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you can only repossess the content of something, but the principle of your dependence remains completely intact.

336
00:25:29,011 --> 00:25:29,972
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot possibly

337
00:25:30,971 --> 00:25:31,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Change.

338
00:25:32,131 --> 00:25:34,812
[SPEAKER_02]: And fully understanding that, means fully understanding Patitsa Upāda.

339
00:25:35,232 --> 00:25:39,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, how you choose to describe Patitsa Upāda, and how many factors and so on, that's actually irrelevant.

340
00:25:40,614 --> 00:25:41,554
[SPEAKER_02]: Or not as relevant.

341
00:25:42,034 --> 00:25:47,935
[SPEAKER_02]: The relevant is the... That's why the Buddha also talked about the nutrient.

342
00:25:49,116 --> 00:25:51,496
[SPEAKER_02]: Contact is the nutrient for the feeling and so on.

343
00:25:51,576 --> 00:25:54,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Not necessarily just the food, the physical food.

344
00:25:55,617 --> 00:25:57,698
[SPEAKER_02]: He's talking about dependence, about support.

345
00:25:58,558 --> 00:26:07,080
[SPEAKER_02]: About simultaneously being determined, as you are, by something that you're not.

346
00:26:07,261 --> 00:26:08,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

347
00:26:08,181 --> 00:26:17,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the functioning body is just a completely... always has to be present and it's completely inseparable from it.

348
00:26:18,304 --> 00:26:19,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And you have no say in it.

349
00:26:19,244 --> 00:26:23,585
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no say in its dependence on further food and so on.

350
00:26:23,805 --> 00:26:26,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, your whole life stands upon it.

351
00:26:27,988 --> 00:26:30,950
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's not something you have to... you can only figure out once.

352
00:26:31,290 --> 00:26:35,873
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to keep drilling and drilling and repeating those contemplations.

353
00:26:37,594 --> 00:26:44,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, through doing that, you keep removing layer by layer of... that you created through ignoring.

354
00:26:46,038 --> 00:26:50,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking, for example, the body is mine, this body is... Yeah, the body is mine and the body eats food.

355
00:26:50,381 --> 00:26:51,181
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's it.

356
00:26:51,341 --> 00:26:52,622
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm eating the food.

357
00:26:52,822 --> 00:26:54,003
[SPEAKER_02]: I have the food, I don't have the food.

358
00:26:54,243 --> 00:26:57,365
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why many people would be so afraid to just go hungry even for a day.

359
00:27:02,024 --> 00:27:04,366
[SPEAKER_02]: You start experiencing genuine anxiety.

360
00:27:04,406 --> 00:27:08,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Just... not... not that you're starving, just for missing a dinner, for example.

361
00:27:11,070 --> 00:27:12,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all assumption.

362
00:27:12,071 --> 00:27:14,492
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a consumption of the world, you know.

363
00:27:14,512 --> 00:27:15,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Consuming the world.

364
00:27:15,993 --> 00:27:16,153
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

365
00:27:16,394 --> 00:27:20,096
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, assumption gives the range for your consumption.

366
00:27:20,536 --> 00:27:20,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

367
00:27:21,317 --> 00:27:22,858
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where sensuality is an assumption.

368
00:27:24,697 --> 00:27:26,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, oh, this is the extent.

369
00:27:27,058 --> 00:27:29,479
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what's determined by the assumption.

370
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:33,081
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where assumption determines the bhāva, determines the being.

371
00:27:34,362 --> 00:27:38,944
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, your consumption is sort of engagement with that extent, with that being.

372
00:27:40,605 --> 00:27:42,346
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you consume, because you assume.

373
00:27:45,067 --> 00:27:46,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you assume that you can consume.

374
00:27:47,489 --> 00:27:50,710
[SPEAKER_02]: You assume that when you eat food, you really assimilated it in you.

375
00:27:51,091 --> 00:27:51,611
[SPEAKER_02]: But you didn't.

376
00:27:52,151 --> 00:27:55,173
[SPEAKER_02]: That food was external to your point of view, when it was on the table.

377
00:27:55,653 --> 00:27:58,034
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's equally external, when it's in this stomach.

378
00:27:58,334 --> 00:28:02,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the stomach and the table are on one plane, and your point of view is on the other.

379
00:28:02,757 --> 00:28:05,378
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is any distinction, it's that.

380
00:28:05,458 --> 00:28:08,420
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like my stomach is in, and the table is outside.

381
00:28:09,652 --> 00:28:14,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything physical is outside, including eating food is still physical and it's outside.

382
00:28:15,238 --> 00:28:23,567
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your dependence on the nutriment means dependence on that external form that you require it to be present and you have no say in.

383
00:28:24,227 --> 00:28:31,755
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, you can be controlling all the food production, and have everything, and monitor your health, and you still will get sick, and you will die.

384
00:28:31,876 --> 00:28:34,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, fundamentally, you cannot have a say in it.

385
00:28:35,600 --> 00:28:41,727
[SPEAKER_02]: It has its own course, it has its own domain, and it's impermeable to you, and to your efforts, and to your intentions.

386
00:28:41,787 --> 00:28:43,148
[SPEAKER_02]: You've taken it in you.

387
00:28:45,198 --> 00:28:51,300
[SPEAKER_01]: When you start looking backwards, so to speak, there is there is no place that you can take it into.

388
00:28:51,480 --> 00:28:52,581
[SPEAKER_02]: No, there is no inside.

389
00:28:52,681 --> 00:28:53,021
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

390
00:28:53,421 --> 00:28:53,801
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

391
00:28:54,662 --> 00:29:03,825
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why for an arahant who has fully removed the slightest traces of those assumptions, there is this body, this conscious body left and name and form externally.

392
00:29:05,025 --> 00:29:07,206
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no more assumption of internal for him.

393
00:29:07,886 --> 00:29:11,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there something about the consciousness doesn't find a footing?

394
00:29:12,911 --> 00:29:14,972
[SPEAKER_02]: No, form doesn't find the footing in the consciousness.

395
00:29:15,732 --> 00:29:18,833
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

396
00:29:18,893 --> 00:29:23,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Footed form is the internal, the assumed internal, in me.

397
00:29:23,375 --> 00:29:26,536
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I am this barrier, and this is outside, and this is in me.

398
00:29:26,596 --> 00:29:28,236
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by having it, I'm taking it in.

399
00:29:29,036 --> 00:29:32,598
[SPEAKER_02]: But, when you start looking, you realize, oh, there is no external and internal.

400
00:29:32,758 --> 00:29:36,059
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can designate it whichever way you want, but it's how you relate to it.

401
00:29:36,179 --> 00:29:39,420
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, internal, it's related to his mind, me, within me.

402
00:29:40,515 --> 00:29:54,363
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you start looking at it in that dispassionate manner, correctly, in the right order, or first with your situation, within that, the dependence of that situation upon something that's simultaneously there, that cannot be yours, then you start seeing there is no external and internal.

403
00:29:54,643 --> 00:29:56,825
[SPEAKER_02]: There is just external and slightly less external.

404
00:29:57,565 --> 00:30:00,267
[SPEAKER_02]: And no matter how far internally you look, it's equally external.

405
00:30:01,807 --> 00:30:03,929
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, it pertains to the external domain.

406
00:30:04,729 --> 00:30:07,070
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just closer to you, so to speak.

407
00:30:09,220 --> 00:30:15,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Before, with that assumption of extent, if it passes that extent, then it's in.

408
00:30:16,403 --> 00:30:19,404
[SPEAKER_02]: You created that internal dimension of mind.

409
00:30:20,364 --> 00:30:27,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you say your self is external, that sense of self... Yeah, of course.

410
00:30:27,847 --> 00:30:29,587
[SPEAKER_02]: Can it be seen as external?

411
00:30:29,847 --> 00:30:32,448
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's when it ceases to be the self.

412
00:30:33,269 --> 00:30:35,049
[SPEAKER_02]: It loses that dimension of mind.

413
00:30:35,409 --> 00:30:36,630
[SPEAKER_02]: It ceases to be atavāda.

414
00:30:38,917 --> 00:30:44,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, that's why also Atavāda is the assumption of a self independent of the five Agri.

415
00:30:44,201 --> 00:30:49,404
[SPEAKER_02]: Independent of the experience as a whole, you assume this greater thing that's outside of it.

416
00:30:49,504 --> 00:30:51,806
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, that it's... all of this is within it.

417
00:30:52,226 --> 00:30:54,428
[SPEAKER_02]: All of this is in me, for me, it's mine.

418
00:30:55,669 --> 00:31:00,312
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so all of those things, all of those things are really revolving around the same principle.

419
00:31:02,459 --> 00:31:06,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how far can you assume?

420
00:31:06,542 --> 00:31:07,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Endlessly, yeah.

421
00:31:07,503 --> 00:31:09,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Endlessly, it's infinite.

422
00:31:09,685 --> 00:31:19,253
[SPEAKER_02]: The assumption of a God... Well, I mean, you can endlessly assume particular, or you can endlessly engage with your assumptions.

423
00:31:20,053 --> 00:31:22,755
[SPEAKER_02]: But, of course, assumptions will come within the certain domains.

424
00:31:23,396 --> 00:31:32,023
[SPEAKER_02]: Assumption of sensuality, assumption of views, assumption of virtue and duty, holding on to it, and so on.

425
00:31:35,586 --> 00:31:41,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of being...

426
00:31:41,707 --> 00:31:45,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to consume the whole universe, isn't it?

427
00:31:45,908 --> 00:31:46,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

428
00:31:47,268 --> 00:31:59,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Assumption is basically maintaining a view of an extent, despite things within that extent proving you're wrong, going otherwise, or being otherwise in nature.

429
00:32:00,691 --> 00:32:01,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Like sensuality, for example.

430
00:32:01,951 --> 00:32:05,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Assumption of sensuality is assumption of the extent of attractive.

431
00:32:07,317 --> 00:32:14,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, in practice, that is always contradicted by the by the by the the repulsive heart of anything sensual.

432
00:32:17,144 --> 00:32:20,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Like repulsive hearts.

433
00:32:20,707 --> 00:32:29,354
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you start seeing that your assumption of sensuality is inseparable from that which is repulsive, inconceivable, apart, bound up with, it ceases to be attractive.

434
00:32:29,514 --> 00:32:33,717
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your assumption has faded without anything being changed, so to speak.

435
00:32:35,448 --> 00:32:36,629
[SPEAKER_02]: All those things still remain.

436
00:32:36,649 --> 00:32:38,070
[SPEAKER_02]: They used to attract you.

437
00:32:38,090 --> 00:32:40,791
[SPEAKER_02]: They used to incite you.

438
00:32:41,372 --> 00:32:43,953
[SPEAKER_02]: But now that incitement and desire is gone.

439
00:32:44,013 --> 00:32:44,954
[SPEAKER_02]: But all those things stay.

440
00:32:45,474 --> 00:32:46,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they were never the problem.

441
00:32:47,115 --> 00:32:50,137
[SPEAKER_02]: It was you assuming a wrong extent in regard to them.

442
00:32:51,437 --> 00:32:54,139
[SPEAKER_02]: You giving them the attractive being through that assumption.

443
00:32:54,339 --> 00:32:56,741
[SPEAKER_02]: They were attractive because you kept assuming them as attractive.

444
00:32:57,081 --> 00:33:04,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you could not see their nutritional dependence upon that which is inherently repulsive.

445
00:33:06,576 --> 00:33:14,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Physical body, food, liquids, smells, anything.

446
00:33:14,022 --> 00:33:16,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Or just general impermanence and lack of control.

447
00:33:16,444 --> 00:33:18,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Aging, sickening, dying.

448
00:33:20,107 --> 00:33:21,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Sensuality is inseparable from it.

449
00:33:21,488 --> 00:33:22,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Any sensuality.

450
00:33:22,188 --> 00:33:23,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Even refined sensuality.

451
00:33:24,050 --> 00:33:28,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Not necessarily just coarse sexuality or gluttony.

452
00:33:28,674 --> 00:33:30,355
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you know, a refined sense of smell.

453
00:33:31,999 --> 00:33:43,827
[SPEAKER_02]: It can even be worse for people, because if they don't see that as equally partaking to the central domain, they won't see where the work is, they won't see where the problem is.

454
00:33:44,828 --> 00:33:50,812
[SPEAKER_02]: So, assumption, by its definition, means taking up for granted.

455
00:33:51,773 --> 00:33:59,839
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this has appeared seemingly attractive, and you just took it at a face value and then started pushing that extent of attractive, well, infinitely.

456
00:34:02,517 --> 00:34:13,179
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, if you start to reverse-engineer it, while it's present, start seeing what's behind it, that keeps determining the extent of your assumption, you realize it's the repulsive.

457
00:34:13,219 --> 00:34:15,679
[SPEAKER_02]: It's that which I cannot control, I have no say in it.

458
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:23,221
[SPEAKER_02]: No amount of beauty can be independent of the material form being present there.

459
00:34:24,201 --> 00:34:30,602
[SPEAKER_02]: And as such, that material form is completely subjected to elements and everything else, that you can't even conceive accessing and controlling.

460
00:34:31,241 --> 00:34:32,381
[SPEAKER_02]: Let alone having is yours.

461
00:34:33,822 --> 00:34:42,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's simply by not thinking about that, you maintain the assumption of sensuality and that whole extent, proliferating extent.

462
00:34:43,364 --> 00:34:50,065
[SPEAKER_02]: If you start thinking about it, that's how you start draining, draining those unwholesome floods, the asuras.

463
00:34:51,186 --> 00:34:53,106
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't just shut off and suddenly stops.

464
00:34:53,206 --> 00:34:56,607
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to find the source and then drain it from the source.

465
00:34:57,727 --> 00:35:00,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Dry it up and then it will evaporate everywhere else.

466
00:35:10,248 --> 00:35:23,614
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a way of trying to gain ultimate control over...

467
00:35:23,694 --> 00:35:28,076
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, it is, it is.

468
00:35:28,196 --> 00:35:32,178
[SPEAKER_02]: It's usually the people's views of God is just extension of their own self.

469
00:35:33,218 --> 00:35:36,159
[SPEAKER_02]: The self-mastery of everything, including yourself.

470
00:35:36,319 --> 00:35:37,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

471
00:35:39,009 --> 00:35:47,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Science, it's basically a new religion for many people.

472
00:35:47,477 --> 00:35:52,662
[SPEAKER_02]: It's this externally adopted view that you cannot have from your point of view.

473
00:35:52,982 --> 00:35:55,905
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only keep assuming it as something that you can have.

474
00:35:56,546 --> 00:35:59,829
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the upādāna of the views, the assumptions of the views,

475
00:36:04,184 --> 00:36:10,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, some meditators are focusing in... Sure.

476
00:36:12,011 --> 00:36:15,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Catch the right moment or witness the atoms and stuff.

477
00:36:15,974 --> 00:36:18,016
[SPEAKER_02]: It's completely the wrong direction.

478
00:36:18,076 --> 00:36:19,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing, isn't it?

479
00:36:19,177 --> 00:36:20,218
[SPEAKER_01]: They try to find...

480
00:36:20,258 --> 00:36:24,722
[SPEAKER_02]: If anything you want... if you want to focus on anything, it should be the background of whatever what's present.

481
00:36:25,462 --> 00:36:28,625
[SPEAKER_02]: But you could see you can't focus on that in the same sense that you used to focus on.

482
00:36:28,705 --> 00:36:30,527
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't concentrate on that as one point.

483
00:36:31,543 --> 00:36:41,506
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, when the Suttas talk about one-pointedness, people usually assume that you get so absorbed in one point that everything else stops bothering you.

484
00:36:41,946 --> 00:36:43,026
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's completely the other way around.

485
00:36:43,586 --> 00:36:49,768
[SPEAKER_02]: You step back into such a general point that everything is included within it, so nothing can bother you.

486
00:36:52,908 --> 00:36:58,432
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, that's why, again, like, what's in common to everything that you can, for example, see visually or experience through your senses?

487
00:36:59,012 --> 00:37:00,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's the four great elements.

488
00:37:01,054 --> 00:37:02,575
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why they're called great elements.

489
00:37:02,635 --> 00:37:10,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because they're particular elements you observe through a microscope, but because you discern the principle of those elements in everything that can appear.

490
00:37:10,381 --> 00:37:20,688
[SPEAKER_02]: If your mind gets established upon the principle of those elements, means you have surmounted anything that's particularly containing that principle.

491
00:37:21,878 --> 00:37:27,202
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, the Buddha would say, upon the greater perceptions of the forest, you abandon perceptions of the village.

492
00:37:28,543 --> 00:37:35,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you realize, well, I can't be bothered by anything from the village, because my mind is established upon this perception of the forest, even when you're not in the forest.

493
00:37:36,250 --> 00:37:40,433
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, I can be bothered upon the things that can arise in the forest, upon those perceptions.

494
00:37:41,474 --> 00:37:46,098
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, he would go further and establish his mind even in a greater background of that.

495
00:37:46,178 --> 00:37:48,239
[SPEAKER_02]: And what's the greater background of village, forest?

496
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:48,940
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the earth.

497
00:37:49,661 --> 00:37:50,922
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the element of earth.

498
00:37:51,763 --> 00:37:54,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, he establishes mindfulness on that refined background.

499
00:37:54,965 --> 00:37:57,987
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can see how general that is, that for most people it's completely abstract.

500
00:37:58,507 --> 00:37:59,308
[SPEAKER_02]: It makes no sense.

501
00:38:00,269 --> 00:38:05,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, for a trained mind, he can perceive it on that abstract level, and that means it's not abstract anymore.

502
00:38:05,592 --> 00:38:10,255
[SPEAKER_02]: It's perceived right and there and then, because it is present, in that general sense.

503
00:38:10,995 --> 00:38:19,081
[SPEAKER_02]: And if the mind gets established upon that, means nothing can bother you, even on account of the perceptions of the forest, let alone those even more inferior perceptions.

504
00:38:19,877 --> 00:38:24,479
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why when we were talking about the casinos, it was exactly that same principle, or jhānas for that matter.

505
00:38:25,539 --> 00:38:33,722
[SPEAKER_02]: You establish your blind upon the greater domain, so that the inferior domain cannot, cannot overcome you, cannot, cannot bother you anymore.

506
00:38:35,375 --> 00:38:38,237
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the casinos will be the four great elements.

507
00:38:38,637 --> 00:38:39,738
[SPEAKER_02]: Any of them can serve.

508
00:38:40,318 --> 00:38:41,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't mean you have to go one, two, three.

509
00:38:41,579 --> 00:38:53,467
[SPEAKER_02]: You take any of it, depending, like, how capable your mind is, and if you establish your mind upon that right order, in terms of the general and more particular things within it, you have surmounted it.

510
00:38:54,248 --> 00:39:00,472
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what the Buddha would say, like, now that the mind is established upon the element of earth, nothing inferior to it can bother me.

511
00:39:01,192 --> 00:39:01,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, oh, is there more?

512
00:39:02,658 --> 00:39:03,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I go even higher?

513
00:39:03,839 --> 00:39:04,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I can.

514
00:39:04,640 --> 00:39:05,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, he refines the element.

515
00:39:06,141 --> 00:39:09,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, he goes into the perceptions of colors and light and so on.

516
00:39:09,863 --> 00:39:10,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the infinite space.

517
00:39:11,305 --> 00:39:21,252
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, eventually, the the casino of consciousness, which is like the most refined, the utmost negative, the utmost background, the utmost general.

518
00:39:22,053 --> 00:39:23,354
[SPEAKER_02]: And he says, there is no higher than this.

519
00:39:26,616 --> 00:39:28,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the casino of consciousness...

520
00:39:31,268 --> 00:39:32,809
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the ultimate background, so to speak.

521
00:39:33,049 --> 00:39:41,953
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, if you say, oh, it's the ultimate background, well, that's now a clearly defined phenomena, a thought, dhamma, in your mind, which means that's not what consciousness is.

522
00:39:42,893 --> 00:39:50,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Being aware of the background to the full extent in regard to whatever has appeared, that's how you develop the casino of consciousness.

523
00:39:52,378 --> 00:39:59,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Being aware of the background without falling into losing mindfulness on account of being pulled and absorbed into particular.

524
00:40:00,756 --> 00:40:10,002
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I would always sort of point out how, like, commonly what people think meditation is, in terms of view and absorption, it's a completely wrong direction.

525
00:40:12,844 --> 00:40:16,047
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to be looking at the background, all what's simultaneously present.

526
00:40:16,647 --> 00:40:21,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Not keep trying to catch the right moment within what's simultaneous, within what's present right in front of you.

527
00:40:22,791 --> 00:40:26,494
[SPEAKER_02]: That all comes from that projection that you can witness something outside of yourself.

528
00:40:27,422 --> 00:40:35,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, whatever you're witnessing, that's your perception, which means, it doesn't matter how far or near you come, perception is there, and you're not seeing its nature.

529
00:40:36,228 --> 00:40:44,754
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we talk about the nature of the five aggregates to be understood, not the particular content that you have to chase down, and catch, and document, and witness.

530
00:40:44,995 --> 00:40:46,115
[SPEAKER_02]: They will then do the work for you.

531
00:40:47,076 --> 00:40:49,778
So, in terms of nāmarūpa, the nutrient for nāmarūpa is

532
00:40:55,965 --> 00:40:56,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

533
00:40:57,326 --> 00:40:57,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

534
00:40:58,847 --> 00:41:00,529
[SPEAKER_02]: The nutriment for nāmarūpa is consciousness.

535
00:41:00,729 --> 00:41:07,214
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, the nutriment for something to appear is the fact that it can appear.

536
00:41:08,455 --> 00:41:10,136
[SPEAKER_02]: And the nutriment for consciousness is nāmarūpa.

537
00:41:10,296 --> 00:41:19,584
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, in order for something to appear, there has to be something that can appear.

538
00:41:19,744 --> 00:41:24,788
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the nāmarūpa determined by consciousness, consciousness determined by nāmarūpa.

539
00:41:27,473 --> 00:41:31,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if there is nothing to appear, you can't even conceive any slightest notion of appearance.

540
00:41:32,398 --> 00:41:36,942
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why there is no consciousness, pure consciousness, or pure awareness, or pure presence in itself.

541
00:41:37,282 --> 00:41:39,164
[SPEAKER_02]: There has to be something that is present.

542
00:41:40,145 --> 00:41:44,089
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where fundamentally rūpa and viññāṇa, or nāmarūpa and viññāṇa, viññāṇa and nāmarūpa.

543
00:41:44,990 --> 00:41:46,711
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't have to go further than that either.

544
00:41:47,912 --> 00:41:52,737
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why in that Dīgha Nikaya Sutta, I can't remember the number, one of the previous Buddha, the Buddha Vipassi,

545
00:41:54,765 --> 00:42:03,975
[SPEAKER_02]: When he was discerning the principle of paticca upādāna, he did not go to the usual description that we know of twelve factors.

546
00:42:03,995 --> 00:42:04,456
[SPEAKER_02]: He stopped.

547
00:42:05,076 --> 00:42:07,238
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, what determines nāmarūpa?

548
00:42:07,319 --> 00:42:08,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's viññāṇa.

549
00:42:10,878 --> 00:42:12,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, then it will go further.

550
00:42:12,319 --> 00:42:13,100
[SPEAKER_02]: What determines viññāṇa?

551
00:42:13,120 --> 00:42:13,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's saṅkhara.

552
00:42:13,741 --> 00:42:14,521
[SPEAKER_02]: What determines saṅkhara?

553
00:42:14,541 --> 00:42:15,722
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's ignorance of saṅkhara.

554
00:42:16,203 --> 00:42:16,623
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's it.

555
00:42:17,023 --> 00:42:19,005
[SPEAKER_02]: But the Buddha Vipassana said, what determines nāmarūpa?

556
00:42:19,045 --> 00:42:19,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Viññāṇa.

557
00:42:19,866 --> 00:42:20,867
[SPEAKER_02]: And what determines viññāṇa?

558
00:42:20,987 --> 00:42:21,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's nāmarūpa.

559
00:42:22,208 --> 00:42:22,548
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

560
00:42:23,168 --> 00:42:24,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, he cannot own it.

561
00:42:25,330 --> 00:42:27,392
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, he cannot take possession of that.

562
00:42:27,712 --> 00:42:30,835
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if he looks at viññāṇa, it's determined by nāmarūpa, so to speak.

563
00:42:30,895 --> 00:42:31,495
[SPEAKER_02]: If he looks at nāmar

564
00:42:34,058 --> 00:42:36,759
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always outside of his reach, the determination.

565
00:42:36,819 --> 00:42:39,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, he fully understood the principle of the determination.

566
00:42:39,700 --> 00:42:44,242
[SPEAKER_02]: The principle of paticampāra, the principle of depending upon something, that it's outside of your reach.

567
00:42:45,503 --> 00:42:49,665
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you remove the notion of ownership, which is a notion of reach.

568
00:42:51,185 --> 00:42:57,508
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he said that he just spent his time then contemplating manifestations of the five aggregates.

569
00:42:59,823 --> 00:43:06,569
[SPEAKER_02]: He went through that principle of being fully determined by that which is outside of his reach, and he became samasambuddha, completely enlightened.

570
00:43:08,370 --> 00:43:11,773
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's... that's why it always comes down to that principle.

571
00:43:11,813 --> 00:43:12,934
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the difference as well.

572
00:43:13,395 --> 00:43:20,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the sutta you read, where you said the first... the first nutriment, you understand one, or you understand the dyad, or you understand the three.

573
00:43:20,521 --> 00:43:24,444
[SPEAKER_02]: It's exactly that difference that Buddha Vipassana didn't go to understanding the one.

574
00:43:25,085 --> 00:43:26,046
[SPEAKER_02]: He came to the dyad.

575
00:43:27,070 --> 00:43:30,111
[SPEAKER_02]: He understood the principle of Bodhisattva Upāda based on it.

576
00:43:30,171 --> 00:43:33,692
[SPEAKER_02]: He contemplated, developed it, seen it through and through, and became Sāmasambuddha.

577
00:43:34,572 --> 00:43:41,034
[SPEAKER_02]: Buddha Gautama was the foremost in formulating the dhamma, which means he was able to discern it up to the fullest.

578
00:43:41,614 --> 00:43:48,816
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why he would formulate the principle of Bodhisattva Upāda in all, like, twelve factors as we know it.

579
00:43:48,876 --> 00:43:53,617
[SPEAKER_02]: But it wasn't a rigid thing, because there's different ways you can describe the same principle.

580
00:43:54,964 --> 00:43:58,587
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why in some other suttas, he will be describing patitsaṁbhāra in sixteen factors.

581
00:43:59,228 --> 00:44:04,773
[SPEAKER_02]: But after feelings and craving, people take up guns and sticks and fight and so on.

582
00:44:04,793 --> 00:44:08,076
[SPEAKER_02]: And it was part of the patitsaṁbhāra.

583
00:44:08,096 --> 00:44:10,878
[SPEAKER_02]: So, don't think about it as some sort of determinational chain.

584
00:44:11,318 --> 00:44:15,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Think about it as a principle of simultaneous presence and dependence upon something that you cannot have.

585
00:44:18,345 --> 00:44:20,847
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it directly undermines the sense of ownership.

586
00:44:23,027 --> 00:44:31,915
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to... the Buddha Vipassana or whoever practices contemplates correctly, doesn't have to sit back and afterwards and then think, how does that prevent me from taking things as mine?

587
00:44:32,695 --> 00:44:41,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding that your sense of self, your entirety of your experience, is determined by that which you cannot have on the same level of that experience.

588
00:44:41,483 --> 00:44:43,845
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at that, then it's determined by the other thing.

589
00:44:44,125 --> 00:44:46,848
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's always... it's always opposite of where you look.

590
00:44:47,508 --> 00:44:57,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, I keep talking about development of mindfulness, development of samādhis through discernment of the background, establishment of your mind upon the background, because that's where determination is.

591
00:44:58,032 --> 00:45:06,495
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't make a determination in front of you and examine it on the same level, because that's something else is the background, which means something else determines that which you're looking at.

592
00:45:08,238 --> 00:45:16,705
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you stop... have to be... you have to cease being focused and concerned with the content and start discerning the nature, the background, the more general.

593
00:45:17,206 --> 00:45:21,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the Buddha named the entire teaching as the nature.

594
00:45:21,129 --> 00:45:26,093
[SPEAKER_02]: There are all these particular dhammas and my teaching is understanding of their nature, understanding of the dhamma.

